      "text_raw": "Another high-pass filter is\nshown in this slide. It uses a\ncapacitor. The transfer char-\nacteristic is very different.\nAt very low frequencies,\nthe gain is constant. It starts\nincreasing at the zero fre-\nquency.\nNote that a first-order\nincreasing characteristic,\nwhich is caused by a zero,\nalways shows a slope of\n20 dB/decade and a 90°\nphase shift.\nObviously, at high fre-\nquencies, the gain will\ndecrease because of the\ninternal poles of the opamp\nitself.",
      "text": "Another high-pass filter is shown in this slide. It uses a capacitor. The transfer characteristic is very different. At very low frequencies, the gain is constant. It starts increasing at the zero frequency. Note that a first-order increasing characteristic, which is caused by a zero, always shows a slope of 20 dB/decade and a 90° phase shift. Obviously, at high frequencies, the gain will decrease because of the internal poles of the opamp itself."
    }
